Kubernetes Readiness Probe vs Liveness Probe — A Common Production Interview Topic

One of the most important concepts in Kubernetes production environments is understanding the difference between Readiness and Liveness probes.

Many application outages occur because probes are configured incorrectly.

✅ Readiness Probe

Determines whether a pod is ready to receive traffic.

If the readiness probe fails:

- Pod remains running
- Pod is removed from the Service endpoints
- No traffic is sent to the pod

Example:
A Spring Boot application takes 60 seconds to start.

Without a readiness probe, traffic may reach the application before it is fully initialized.

---

✅ Liveness Probe

Determines whether the application is still healthy.

If the liveness probe fails:

- Kubernetes restarts the container automatically

Example:
Application gets stuck due to a deadlock or memory issue.

The liveness probe detects the unhealthy state and triggers a restart.

---

Example Configuration:

livenessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /health
    port: 8080
  initialDelaySeconds: 30

readinessProbe:
  httpGet:
    path: /ready
    port: 8080
  initialDelaySeconds: 10

---

Real Production Scenario:

We once had an application where pods showed:

STATUS: Running

But users were receiving errors.

Root cause:
👉 The application was running, but it was not ready to serve requests.

After implementing a proper readiness probe:
✅ Traffic was routed only to healthy pods
✅ Deployment stability improved
✅ User-facing errors were reduced

---

📌 Interview Tip:

A common question is:

"What's the difference between Readiness and Liveness Probes?"

A good answer:

✔ Readiness → Controls traffic flow to the pod
✔ Liveness → Controls container restart behavior

Understanding this difference is critical for running reliable applications in Kubernetes.
